{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"source /public/home/shiys/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate easynco && sed -n '1,260p' code/V4/runs/R63_recurrent_workspaces/reviews/R63_closure_R64_plan.response.md\"","aggregated_output":"`review_independence=same-family; acceptance_status=provisional`\n\nR63 performance readiness: **4/10, NOT READY for the 70% objective.** R64 plan readiness: **8/10, ready for the bounded experiment below only.** I read the complete reviewer memory first, then the completed TRAIN/validation artifacts. No new TEST results or predictions were inspected; no files were edited or GPU work launched.\n\nThe selected results are:\n\n| Model | Completed epochs / updates | Selected epoch | TRAIN Top1 | Validation Top1 | Validation regret |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| [R63A](/public/home/shiys/0selection2/code/V4/runs/R63_recurrent_workspaces/A_static_memory_seed2/result.json) | 40 / 56,880 | 34 | 50.4822% | 48.4944% | 0.976334% |\n| [R63B](/public/home/shiys/0selection2/code/V4/runs/R63_recurrent_workspaces/B_communicating_memory_seed2/result.json) | 15 / 21,330 | 12 | 47.4606% | 47.3444% | 0.995003% |\n| [R60B reference](/public/home/shiys/0selection2/code/V4/runs/R60_expanded_partial_supervision/B_expanded_seed2/result.json) | 40 / 56,880 | 39, cost-selected | 50.7706% | 49.3611% | 0.922789% |\n\nCorresponding validation macro mean costs are 11.175508, 11.177965 and 11.168642. Neither R63 arm improves the historical reference.\n\nThe final A40/B15 difference **cannot identify a communication effect**. Within the matched first 15 epochs, best validation Top1 is A 47.1889% versus B 47.3444%, only **+0.1556pp** for B. At epoch 15 itself, B leads by 0.4389pp. This establishes no substantial communication benefit and does not establish communication harm.\n\nB’s [execution accounting](/public/home/shiys/0selection2/code/V4/runs/R63_recurrent_workspaces/B_communicating_memory_seed2/wall_time.json) is **13,051.80s interrupted execution + 96.61s zero-update finalization**, totaling 13,148.41s. The boundary receipt places interruption after complete epoch-15 TRAIN/validation and checkpoint publication, before epoch 16; no discarded partial training is reported. The finalization launcher spans approximately 144s, including about 47.39s outside the arm timer. A records 35,202.47s; combined recorded arm wall is **13.4308 hours**, excluding preparation and launcher overhead. Finalization is not additional training.\n\nCached-input singleton selection averages approximately **9.46/9.44ms** for A/B. These measurements include graph construction and output transfer but exclude disk decoding, geometry-cache preparation, checkpoint loading and solver execution.\n\nOne actual implementation defect needs disclosure: epochs 32 and 34 both have **8,729/18,000** correct validation predictions. Floating-point macro averaging produces `.4849444444444444` versus `.4849444444444445`, so [the comparison](/public/home/shiys/0selection2/code/V4/r63_experiment.py:352) violates the stated earliest-on-tie rule. Preserve the recorded R63 checkpoint and any frozen test choice; fix R64 prospectively using integer correct counts. This does not change R63’s reported Top1 or performance verdict.\n\nI recommend **PCGrad on the shared dense classifier core**, with ordinary gradient averaging over identical mixed-task batches as the sole control.\n\nR63’s selected TRAIN/validation gaps are only 1.9878pp and 0.1161pp. Severe memorization is therefore not the leading diagnosis for this recipe. R59–R62 transfer attempts and R63’s additional computation also leave shared optimization largely unexplored. Cross-task interference remains a **project hypothesis**, not an observed cause: heterogeneous task accuracy alone cannot establish it. The PCGrad paper explicitly distinguishes negative gradient alignment from conditions making interference harmful; its algorithm and [official implementation](https://github.com/tianheyu927/PCGrad/blob/master/PCGrad_tf.py) provide a concrete intervention. Their results do not predict this project’s accuracy. [Primary paper, §§2.2–2.3](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2020/file/3fe78a8acf5fda99de95303940a2420c-Paper.pdf).\n\nImplement R64 as follows:\n\n1. **Arms and architecture.** `A_mean_gradient_seed2` and `B_pcgrad_seed2`, from identical fresh seed-2 parameters. Use the R45A dual-stream backbone: d128, four instance-encoder layers, two joint layers, four heads, FFN512, score-head hidden256, dropout .1, existing handcrafted solver features and TRAIN-fitted local geometry. Preserve all task/constraint inputs and the full directed ATSP matrix. Call `encode_pairs` and the existing `score_head` to obtain **direct class logits**; bypass maximin conversion. Both arms contain the same complete model, without recurrent workspaces, pretrained weights or task experts.\n\n2. **Data and objective.** Original 10,000 TRAIN queries for each of all 18 tasks, unchanged splits, native winners, 22 identities and all 128 legal deployments. For each task minibatch use `L = .5*(CE(u1,y)+CE(u2,y)) + lambda*symKL(u1,u2)`, with two independent dropout forwards. Ramp `lambda` linearly from zero to .35 over the first three epochs. Use the existing legal-pool symmetric-KL convention. No class balancing, cost weighting, expanded supervision or auxiliary targets. Labels and routes remain outside forward inputs.\n\n3. **Batch/update semantics.** Reuse `paired_schedule(..., batch_size=42, seed=2)`. Each epoch has 239 cycles; each cycle shuffles all 18 distinct tasks and partitions them into six consecutive triples. Each task contributes its next 42 examples, or its final four. Compute the three task gradients at the **same parameter state**, then take one optimizer step. Thus each epoch covers exactly 180,000 queries in **1,434 optimizer updates**; effective batch is normally 126, with final triples of 12. This keeps update counts close to the earlier 1,422/epoch while allowing direct task-gradient comparison. Both arms have identical triples, examples and dropout draws.\n\n4. **The sole intervention.** Flatten gradients over these fixed shared prefixes: `instance_encoder.layers`, `solver_encoder.feature_mlp`, `solver_encoder.norm`, `joint_layers`, `pool`, `decoder`, `score_head`. A applies their arithmetic mean. For B, start `v_i=g_i`; visit the other two **original** gradients in independently shuffled order, applying `v_i -= min(v_i·g_j,0)/(g_j·g_j)*g_j`, skipping zero norms. Average the resulting three vectors. All remaining parameters use ordinary mean gradients in both arms. Do not normalize individual gradients or project weight decay. Use a separate projection-order RNG seeded 6402, isolated from sampling/dropout RNG.\n\n5. **Numerics and optimizer.** Form finite, unscaled FP32 gradient buffers before projection; clip the final complete gradient once to norm 1. Take exactly one AdamW step, LR `1e-4`, weight decay `1e-4`, betas `(0.9,0.999)`, epsilon `1e-8`. Retain existing AMP practice, but any nonfinite task gradient discards and retries the **entire triple**, restoring its RNG and reducing scale. Do not advance optimizer state, sample cursors or successful-update counts on failure; do not unscale twice.\n\n6. **Schedule and checkpoint selection.** Warm up LR linearly over `3*1434` successful updates. A then controls the common epoch LR trace and horizon: validation native Top1, .001pp effective improvement, LR patience3/factor.5, stop patience8, min15/max40 epochs. B replays exactly A’s horizon and schedule. Maximum is **57,360 updates and 7.2 million query presentations per arm**. Select each arm’s strict highest validation native macro Top1, retaining the earliest exact tie. Since all validation tasks have 1,000 examples, compare integer total correct counts; cost never breaks ties.\n\n7. **Evaluation and test freeze.** One eval-mode FP32 forward over the full legal pool; select the highest direct logit, ties by stable global solver ID. Exactly one solver is selected, with no retrieval, ensemble or per-task model choice. After both arms complete, the parent freezes one validation-selected checkpoint per arm, the inference rule, and the official arm by validation Top1, tie A. Run each frozen model’s full test once. Test cannot change checkpoint, arm, rules or future mechanism selection; disclose historical test exposure.\n\n8. **Reporting and execution.** During normal training record task losses, gradient norms, conflict frequency, projection-change magnitude, clipping and retries. Report full18/family/per-task TRAIN and validation curves, native Top1/2/3, regret, mean cost, SBS comparisons, selection distributions and required plots; add locked test reporting afterward. Measure actual wall, memory and singleton/batched inference during ordinary evaluation. Only brief CPU checks for projection, batch coverage and exact ties precede formal training. Sol uses `easynco`, tmux and only `GPU-cb626bdb-4319-2f5e-b91f-17fcaa44a83d`; no timing pilot or review campaign. Commit once after the whole version completes.\n\n**Failure condition:** if B’s selected full18 validation Top1 does not exceed A’s, this fixed PCGrad recipe has no demonstrated selection benefit. Lower training loss without higher validation Top1 is only an optimization result. Negligible gradient corrections would weaken the proposed interference explanation. Any small gain remains provisional single-seed evidence; **70% remains the objective**, with no regret, cost or mechanism-margin veto.\n\nMemory update, proposed here without modifying the file: R63 is completed under the authorized unequal A40/B15 budgets; B finalization added zero updates. Common-prefix communication gain is small, while final comparisons are budget-confounded. Current artifacts weaken the severe-overfitting diagnosis but do not establish gradient interference or an accuracy ceiling. The floating-point checkpoint-tie defect is confirmed and requires only a prospective count-based fix. R60B remains the validation reference. R64 tests one shared-gradient coordination mechanism against matched averaging. R58 and eight B-task semantics remain unresolved by user deferral; no new TEST evidence informed this recommendation, and the full18 single-solver 70% goal remains unachieved.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
